---
/**
 * /principles — how this site decides what to propose: the four principles
 * and the four ownership rules, each as a first-class item with the chapter
 * that argues it (IA §1.1). It does not retell the demographic story
 * (IA §5 row 11: the frame stays a pointer) and it does not restate the
 * ownership argument (§5 row 2: the four rules as a list plus links to the
 * two canonical homes).
 *
 * Moved onto ArticleLayout (DESIGN.md Migration step 3, E006-T19): the
 * 52rem/max-w-6xl frame and the card grids are gone; the page consumes the
 * 66ch `--article-measure` column through ArticleGrid like every other
 * long-form route.
 *
 * Every sentence below traces to .plan/vision/aging-society-frame.md
 * (dictated 2026-09-20): the layers :39-42, :85-86; the language floor
 * :53-57, :67-70; old-age provision :96-98; who captures the gains
 * :113-120, :155-162; how it happens :166-169; who pays :175-177; the
 * political position :211-226. The 2024-25 principles prose ("empathetic
 * cooperation over egocentric competition", "blockchain-based contracts",
 * "a testament to the power of innovation") had no dictation behind it and
 * is retired (src/AGENTS.md §2, "Corporate register").
 */
import ArticleLayout from '../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Lead from '../components/Lead.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Principles"
  description="How this site decides what to propose: four principles and four ownership rules, each with the chapter that argues it, once the demographic frame is accepted."
  backHref="/vision"
  backLabel="Back to Vision Overview"
>
  <Fragment slot="meta">
    <Lead
      paragraphs={[
        'Every proposal on this site is tested against the same few rules. This page states them, in the order the frame sets — a working language, then a digital state, then robotics — and points at the chapter where each one is argued.',
      ]}
      claim="Nothing here is the argument itself. It is the ruler the arguments are measured with."
    />
  </Fragment>

  <Section eyebrow="The frame" title="Why now">
    <p>
      The demographic case for acting now — the shared condition, the window of roughly
      thirty years we still have to build the systems that will carry us, and the shift
      from relying on more workers to relying on automation — is laid out in full in
      <a href="/book/designing-our-retirement">Designing Our Retirement</a>. What follows
      is not that argument again; it is the rules we apply once we accept it.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="How we decide" title="The four principles" lead="Each one is a sentence from the frame, and each has a chapter that carries it.">
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Everyone wins, or it does not pass.</strong> A proposal built so that
          everyone wins can earn the unanimity that treaty change requires; treaty change is
          then a question of political will, which follows conviction. The way to find such
          proposals is to start from what almost all of us already agree on and work that
          out properly, instead of hunting for the line that divides.
          <a href="/book/political-path">The Political Path</a> argues the first half;
          <a href="/manifesto#the-method">the manifesto</a> names the second as a method.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Pay once, together.</strong> Every member state funds its own
          digitalisation separately. Doing it together is not a new budget; it is a less
          wasteful use of the one we have.
          <a href="/book/designing-our-retirement#who-pays-for-it">Who pays for it</a>, in
          the frame chapter.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>One working language makes one public.</strong> European political
          consciousness cannot exist without a shared language: the awareness people build
          working together in business becomes, over time, the awareness they bring to
          European politics. A shared interest is what keeps us from turning on each
          other; the Union was built so there would be no more war, and a common venture
          that stays worth more than any quarrel is how that works in this century.
          <a href="/book/one-working-language">One Working Language</a> is the floor of
          the whole plan.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Automate old-age provision so the few young people left are not crushed
          carrying it.</strong> Robotics is the top of the stack, not the starting point; it
          needs the layers beneath it, and the targets come in the order they are ready —
          municipal services, food production and delivery, predictive medicine.
          <a href="/book/robotic-reindustrialisation">Robotic Reindustrialisation</a>
          argues where the machines go first.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Runs through every proposal" title="Who captures the gains" lead="Private capital, or everyone. Every proposal on this site has to answer it.">
    <p>
      If automation replaces the labour our pensions depend on and the returns go to a
      handful of owners, we have solved production and destroyed the distribution that made
      it worth solving. Settling ownership first is the wrong order; the models will be
      mixed. What matters more is that the technology stays cheap enough that it cannot be
      enclosed:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>cheap to buy and run;</li>
      <li>available to every municipality, farm and clinic;</li>
      <li>built on open standards;</li>
      <li>hard to monopolise.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Why cheapness, not ownership, is the goal is argued in
      <a href="/book/cheap-is-wealth">Cheap Is Wealth</a>. Who the surplus has to reach is
      argued in
      <a href="/book/who-it-earns-for#it-has-to-earn-for-society-not-only-for-capital">Who It Earns For</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Where this stands" title="Pro-European, not attached to the present shape">
    <p>
      The current administration is rejected by a large part of the public, and it is not
      the point. What is worth defending is the project underneath: free nations with enough
      shared interest that war stays unthinkable. If the institutions have to change form to
      do that, they change form — the shape proposed here is
      <a href="/book/direct-democracy#the-union-of-nations">a Union of Nations</a>, rebuilt
      from the inside.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a manifesto and a programme, not a party platform, and it does not need to be
      politically correct. It needs to be worth reading. The demand it makes of you is on
      <a href="/manifesto">the manifesto</a>; the full plan is on
      <a href="/vision">the vision page</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</ArticleLayout>
